WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.490
While kids absolutely adore Marvel movies,
there's a lot about this franchise that only

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.820
grown-ups will appreciate.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.960
From tacky outfits and glossed-over casualties
to an incessant barrage of quick-witted innuendos,

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.000
here are some things in the MCU that only
adults will notice.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.160
Between the mythological gods, radioactive
monsters, and talking racoons, it's pretty

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.100
obvious that no one is going into a Marvel
movie looking for a dose of realism.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:28.160
When it comes to space travel, though, believability
gets stretched pretty far, even by the goofiest

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.160
sci-fi standards.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.400
At this point, a fair number of humans have
been shown making trips through space.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.570
Sometimes this has very real consequences,
like when Tony Stark nearly died from a lack

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.620
of oxygen, or when Peter Parker had to be
saved before he was yanked out of the atmosphere

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:42.880
by the Black Order's spaceship.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.870
At other times, though, any of the problems
with being in space or on an alien planet

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.000
are completely ignored.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.340
When our very human heroes get to Thanos's
home world of Titan in Avengers: Infinity

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.360
War, they're able to breathe just fine on
an alien planet.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:59.260
The same goes for Vormir when Hawkeye and
Black Widow head over there in Endgame to

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.080
find everything hunky-dory, physiologically
speaking.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.400
The movies spend a lot of time on the pseudo-science
that explains all of the strange phenomena

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.930
throughout the MCU, but space travel seems
to operate entirely on whatever seems convenient

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:11.600
for the plot.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.960
Kids might not notice, but if you're old enough
to have taken a science class, or even read

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.050
the original Infinity Gauntlet comics, where
suffocating in the vacuum of space is a pretty

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:24.040
big concern during these superhero battles,
it's an easy detail to get hung up on.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.000
In most of their films, the Marvel masterminds
have managed to keep a core of something believable

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.360
within the context of their very unbelievable
stories.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.659
Captain America: Civil War centers on a very
realistic showdown built around relatable

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.600
ideas of loyalty and betrayal, even if it
also involves flying androids, robotic power

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.800
armor, and teenagers with spider-strength.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.410
In spite of it all, the serious tone of the
film draws the audience past all that and

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.280
into that emotional core.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.180
That doesn't change the fact that many of
the superheroic struggles taking place throughout

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.880
the MCU are ridiculously lopsided, however.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.660
Take the fight with Thanos on Titan.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.880
Endgame shows us that he's a villain that
can hold his own against super-powerful characters

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.770
like Captain Marvel, even without the Infinity
Gauntlet, but in Infinity War, the Mad Titan

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.420
spends several minutes struggling against
characters like Drax and Star-Lord.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.020
Sure, Drax is strong, but he's not strong
enough to destroy an entire fleet of spaceships

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.300
with his bare hands, and Peter Quill is really
just a guy with laser guns and a comprehensive

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.480
knowledge of classic rock.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.260
They're not exactly the biggest threats.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.020
It's not just that fight scene, either.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.360
You can see the wildly imbalanced super-powers
on display just by looking at the Avengers.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.950
On the one hand, you have characters like
Black Widow and Hawkeye, who are incredibly

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.500
skilled and deadly by regular human standards.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.920
On the other hand, you have the Hulk, who
can punch out space monsters the size of a

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.240
battleship, and Thor, the literal god of Thunder.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.240
Kids like to see all of their favorite standing
shoulder-to-shoulder, but as adults, we have

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.740
to ask: is Clint Barton really contributing
a whole lot to the fight?

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.960
It doesn't matter if you're witnessing the
epic climax of the film, or even a fight between

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.180
the heroes themselves, superheroic battles
are almost always the focus of these movies.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.180
The consequences of those battles, however,
are all over the map.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.940
Sometimes the lasting effects of a major encounter
play an important part of the story, like

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.580
the destruction of Sokovia in Avengers: Age
of Ultron.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.220
That one is treated as a massive tragedy with
huge ramifications for the rest of the universe.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.600
For every scene like this, however, there
are a dozen that never really bring up all

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:18.739
the collateral damage that comes along with
trashing armies of alien drones or swinging

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.020
around magic hammers.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.050
The Battle of New York in Avengers, for instance,
is shown to be so devastating that the World

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:28.599
Security Council is willing to nuke Manhattan,
which has to indicate a ridiculous number

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.420
of casualties.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.320
And yet, once the credits roll, it's never
brought up again.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.250
The same goes for the end of Captain America:
The Winter Soldier, where S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:40.600
Helicarriers are dropping out of the sky over
Washington, DC and into the Potomac river.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.520
These are incredibly destructive events in
highly populated areas, and as adults, that's

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.360
the kind of thing we tend to think about once
we're out of the theater.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.780
Here's a fun fact: if you're under the age
of, say, 18, then you've never really experienced

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.840
a pop cultural landscape that didn't feature
the MCU.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.920
The characters, on the other hand, seem pretty
well-acquainted with the movies that hit theaters

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.980
long before Tony Stark built himself a suit
of super-armor in a cave…even the younger

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:05.480
ones.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:06.620
"Hey guys!

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.020
You ever see that really old movie…Empire
Strikes Back?"

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.040
"Jesus, Tony, how old is this guy?!"

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.220
"I don't know, I didn't carbon date him.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.140
He's on the young side."

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:18.240
The superheroes of the Marvel Universe are
always throwing out references to classic

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:22.379
movies, from Peter Parker's love of the Star
Wars trilogy to Tony Stark referring to Thor

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.100
as "Point Break."

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.240
We've even seen Captain Marvel crashing down
to Earth in a Blockbuster Video, which, for

00:04:27.249 --> 00:04:30.870
those youngsters among you, was sort of like
a Netflix that you had to physically drive

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.309
to, and you could only watch the movie you
wanted if you got there before everyone else

00:04:34.309 --> 00:04:35.000
got off work.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.780
That's exactly the kind of reference that
today's kids just aren't going to understand.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.780
We've come a long way from the X-Men films
of the early 2000s, where the characters swapped

00:04:43.789 --> 00:04:47.689
out their familiar, brightly colored comic
book costumes for allegedly "functional" black

00:04:47.689 --> 00:04:48.640
leather outfits.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.960
"You actually go outside in these things?"

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:52.460
"What would you prefer?

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:53.960
Yellow spandex?"

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.820
These days, we've all gotten used to seeing
superheroes showing up on screen looking just

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.960
like their source material, but that doesn't
change the fact that their outfits are often

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.260
straight-up ridiculous.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:05.920
That doesn't mean they look bad, though.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.920
Without those colorful suits, the MCU would
wind up being a pretty drab place.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.580
It's just that we never quite get why nobody
chooses to dress in something a little more

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.500
practical.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:15.900
Just look at Steve Rogers.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.770
His original costume is meant to be, well,
a costume.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.249
It's specifically made for his on-stage appearances
selling war bonds.

00:05:22.249 --> 00:05:26.360
When he goes into action, however, he dresses
more like a soldier…for about five minutes.

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:30.260
He's then given a slightly more functional
version of his stage costume, and for some

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.480
reason sticks with it when he wakes up in
the 21st century, too.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.840
Even when he goes underground after the events
of Civil War, he keeps the same suit and just

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.360
takes off all the white parts to go with a
cool new beard.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.540
For a kid, all that makes sense: Cap looks
like that because that's just what Captain

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:45.100
America looks like.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.120
For the cynical, critical adult eyes, though,
we have to wonder why he doesn't just dress

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.409
in a more tactical outfit.

00:05:50.409 --> 00:05:54.389
And, for that matter, why Tony Stark doesn't
just build everyone on the team a suit of

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:57.779
armor, especially the ones who are very vulnerable
to getting shot.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:58.780
"You can't have it."

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.120
Captain America: Civil War is a great movie.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.710
It has one of the franchise's most memorable
battles, introduces not one, but two brand-new

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.599
major characters to the MCU, picks up on plot
threads from Age of Ultron, and ties together

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:15.619
the entire Marvel Cinematic Universe while
still focusing on its title character and

00:06:15.619 --> 00:06:17.500
how far he's willing to go to help his friends.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:21.400
It also seems on the surface like it's a perfect
movie for kids to get excited about.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.480
After all, Spider-Man has always been a favorite
for younger fans, and what kid wouldn't be

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.869
excited to see Ant-Man growing up to giant
size during that unforgettable airport battle

00:06:29.869 --> 00:06:30.360
in Germany?

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.360
The rest of the movie, on the other hand,
might not be ideal for that particular audience.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.229
In addition to being built around the fact
that Captain America's best friend just straight-up

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:42.509
murdered Iron Man's mom and dad, most of the
plot of Civil War is a debate about government

00:06:42.509 --> 00:06:43.060
oversight.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:47.740
As exciting as it is to see Captain America
bicep-curling a helicopter, having two characters

00:06:47.749 --> 00:06:52.000
argue over whether they should submit to authority
or remain independent in order to follow their

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.169
own moral compass and pursue a more proactive,
interventionist policy is…well, it's not

00:06:56.169 --> 00:06:58.760
exactly what the elementary school set is
excited about.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:02.400
And that's before you get to the part where
the main villain is doing everything simply

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.800
because he craves death.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:04.620
Yikes.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.900
Fortunately, anyone who was a little too young
for all that probably just remembers how cool

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.169
it was when Ant-Man rode on Hawkeye's arrow.

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:12.160
It's just like the comics!

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.740
It's not unusual for a movie that appeals
to kids to throw in some jokes for the grown-ups

00:07:16.749 --> 00:07:20.809
in the audience, and with the kind of cross-generational
appeal that has led to record-breaking box

00:07:20.809 --> 00:07:23.640
office totals, the MCU movies are no exception.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:28.349
The thing is, in these movies, all those "adult"
jokes are, well, really adult, especially

00:07:28.349 --> 00:07:30.360
when they're coming from Tony Stark and Peter
Quill.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.640
Iron Man always has a sarcastic retort ready
for any situation, like when he asked Black

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:38.330
Widow and Hulk if they were playing "hide
the zucchini," or when he took a swipe at

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.800
Hawkeye for having a little difficulty lifting
up Mjolnir.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.460
"We won't hold it against you if you can't
get it up."

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.720
Star-Lord, on the other hand, makes Tony look
like he could be teaching Sunday School.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.960
In a movie that features a talking raccoon,
you know, for the kids…

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:53.080
"Oh.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.100
Yeah."

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.219
...the Guardians' leader mentions that his
starship would look a lot like a Jackson Pollack

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.740
painting underneath a blacklight.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.660
If you're an adult, you already get it.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.860
And that's just the tip of the innuendo iceberg.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.480
Fortunately, these are exactly the kind of
jokes that are going to go right over the

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.080
kids' heads.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.440
Unless, of course, they have reflexes like
Drax.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.660
"Nothing goes over my head.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.840
My reflexes are too fast, I would catch it."

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:26.349
If the past ten years have proven anything,
it's that kids love Iron Man.

00:08:26.349 --> 00:08:28.029
There are plenty of reasons why, too.

00:08:28.029 --> 00:08:32.830
He's a modern-day knight in literal shining
armor, with a seemingly endless personal fortune

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:35.800
and enough smarts to literally invent a new
element in his basement.

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:40.000
He's the cool exec with a heart of steel,
and hey, Spider-Man likes him, too, so all

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.320
the other kids should too, right?

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:42.620
Not exactly.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:45.400
"You missed me?

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:46.900
I missed you too.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:47.440
Blow something up?

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:48.820
I already did that."

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:53.400
Tony Stark's entire character arc over the
course of ten years of movies is built around

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.560
some very adult-oriented themes, many of which
might be missed by younger viewers who are

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.440
busy focusing on the glitz and glamor of the
Iron Man armor.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:04.520
The man started as a playboy who was constantly
womanizing and drinking without a care in

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:05.020
the world.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.680
He treated people like garbage and didn't
blink twice at the moral struggle of living

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.010
off of a fortune built on dealing weapons.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.760
Even after he made his change and started
to reforge his personality into one of the

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:19.720
greatest superheroes in the entire MCU, his
story remained just as adult in nature.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.660
In Age of Ultron, he dealt with a world that
was suddenly a whole lot bigger than he thought

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:28.390
by making huge mistakes, and Iron Man 3 is
a movie entirely about post-traumatic stress

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:28.880
disorder.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:33.140
Even in his final moments, his inner struggles
continued until he accepted that he'd have

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:37.560
to sacrifice his own life, and any chance
of seeing his daughter grow up, to save everyone

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.060
else.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.380
Without a doubt, the quantity of high-minded,
adult level material in the Stark storyline

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:43.160
is overwhelming.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:43.880
But hey!

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:44.960
That armor's pretty cool, right?

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.020
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.240
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:55.560
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

